1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back. When one example isn't enough, the reflex is to keep adding more.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
And that reflex is usually wrong.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This lesson is about multi-shot prompting, using two or more examples.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And the thing that actually drives results isn't how many you show.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
It's how well you choose them. Let's break it down.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
So, what does multi-shot actually do?

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
That one example can't.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
With several worked examples, you're no longer showing the model a single instance.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
You're showing it a pattern it can generalize from.

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Several examples reinforce an output format across different cases,

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
so the model sees it isn't a one-off.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
They demonstrate a tone, a structure, or even a reasoning style you want imitated.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
And repetition across examples makes that style stick.

14
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Most usefully, multiple examples show the model how to handle variation,

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
the differences between one input and the next that a single example would hide.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
That's the real payoff.

17
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
One example teaches a case.

18
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Several examples teach the underlying rule.

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
If example choice is what matters, what makes an example good? Three marks.

20
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,000
First, relevant.

21
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The example should mirror your actual use case closely,

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
because the pattern it teaches only helps if it transfers to the real inputs you'll send.

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Second, diverse.

24
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Your examples should vary enough to cover the edge cases.

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Otherwise, the model overfits to one narrow shape and stumbles the moment reality looks different.

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Third, structured.

27
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Each example needs to be clearly separated from your instructions, usually with delimiters.

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
So the model reads it as a sample to imitate, rather than a command to follow.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Relevant, diverse, and clearly marked.

30
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Hit those three and a small set of examples does a lot of work.

31
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Here's the single most important idea in this lesson.

32
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Quality beats quantity. Every time.

33
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:08,000
The instinct when few-shot isn't working is to add more examples, but piling on usually backfires.

34
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,000
A handful of diverse, well-chosen examples will outperform a long list of near-duplicates,

35
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
because duplicates teach the model the same thing over and over, while covering nothing new.

36
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The rule I use is that every example should earn its place by teaching something the others don't.

37
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
If a new example just restates a case you've already shown, it's adding token cost with zero added coverage.

38
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Anthropic's own guidance points the same way.

39
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:44,000
A small, carefully chosen set of examples, not an exhaustive dump of every case you can think of.

40
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Let's talk about where your examples actually go.

41
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Because placement trips people up.

42
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Few-shot examples live in the message history.

43
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
You put them in as prior turns, a sample input, and the output you'd want, sitting ahead of your

44
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
real request, so the model reads them first.

45
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Now, one older technique you might have heard of was prefilling the assistant's turn to force a particular format,

46
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
essentially starting the model's answer for it.

47
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
On current Claude models, that assistant prefill is rejected outright, so it's no longer a valid trick.

48
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
The modern replacement is to lean on your few-shot examples for shaping.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
And when you need a strict format, reach for structured output instead of trying to force the opening tokens.

50
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Let me frame the tradeoff at the heart of example choice.

51
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Coverage versus clutter.

52
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Good coverage means your examples span the real input range, including the tricky edge cases the

53
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
model would otherwise fumble.

54
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:47,000
When your set covers that range, the model generalizes well, because it has seen the shape of the

55
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
hard cases, not just the easy ones.

56
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Clutter is the opposite failure.

57
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
It's when you stack up many similar examples of the simple case.

58
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
That bloats the prompt, wastes tokens on every call, and can actually overfit the model to the happy path.

59
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
So it does great on the obvious inputs, and falls apart on anything unusual.

60
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Aim for coverage of the range, not repetition of the easy win.

61
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
There's a point where adding examples stops paying off, and recognizing it saves you tokens.

62
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Examples showed diminishing returns.

63
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The first few carry most of the value, teaching format and covering the main variations.

64
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
After that, each new example does less.

65
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Once your set already covers the input range, another example mostly repeats what the model has seen.

66
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
So it adds cost without adding coverage.

67
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And that cost is real.

68
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Every example rides along in the prompt on every single call, inflating your input tokens.

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The practical signal is to watch output quality as you add examples.

70
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
When quality flattens out and a new example no longer changes the result, you've hit the ceiling.

71
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Stop there, and trim anything that isn't earning its place.

72
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Let's pull the placement rules together, because getting them right is half the battle.

73
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Put your few-shot examples in the message history, as prior input and output turns that come before the real request.

74
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Delimit each one clearly, so the model can tell where an example ends and your instructions begin,

75
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and reads it as a sample to imitate, rather than a directive.

76
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
And skip the Assistant Prefill approach entirely, since current models reject it.

77
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:35,000
If what you really need is a rigid output format, the cleaner path is Structured Output,

78
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
which constrains the response shape directly.

79
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Pair a few well-chosen examples with Structured Output,

80
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and you get reliable formatting without stuffing the prompt full of redundant demonstrations.

81
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Let me leave you with three habits that keep few-shot prompting clean.

82
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
First, curate, don't dump.

83
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Choose a small set of canonical examples that clearly portray the behavior you want,

84
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
instead of stuffing in every case you can imagine.

85
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Second, test the set.

86
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Look at your examples honestly and ask whether each one is relevant and diverse,

87
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
then drop any that don't add new coverage.

88
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
You can even ask the model itself to check them for you.

89
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Third, match the placement.

90
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Keep examples as prior turns and delimit them,

91
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
so the model treats them as demonstrations, rather than stray instructions.

92
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Curate, test, and place well,

93
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:33,000
and a handful of examples will outperform a bloated prompt every time. Let's recap.

94
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Multi-shot gives you a pattern to generalize from,

95
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
but the win is choosing examples well, not counting them.

96
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Relevant, diverse examples beats a pile of duplicates.

97
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Curate a small canonical set,

98
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
test whether each example adds new coverage,

99
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
and keep them delimited as prior turns.

100
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
That wraps up multi-shot prompting and example choice.

101
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Let's keep going in the next video.

